#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_gudermannian

#% text
# `inverse_gudermannian(x)` gives the inverse Gudermannian function log(tan(x/2 + pi/4)).

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The branch is that of the principal logarithm: `inverse_gudermannian(z) = log(tan(z/2 + pi/4))`, which equals 2 atanh(tan(z/2)) everywhere except on the real intervals where |tan(x/2)| > 1.

#% item
# The function is real on the intervals -pi/2 < x - 2 pi k < pi/2 and has period 2 pi.

#% item
# On the real intervals pi/2 < x - 2 pi k < 3 pi/2 the value is 2 acoth(tan(x/2)) + pi i: the imaginary part is +pi on both sides of each singular point, and `gudermannian(inverse_gudermannian(x)) = x` for real x in (-pi, pi).

#% item
# `inverse_gudermannian(gudermannian(x)) = x` for real x.

#% item
# `inverse_gudermannian(-z) = -inverse_gudermannian(z)` except on the real intervals with imaginary part pi; exact and numeric evaluation follow the same branch.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_gudermannian(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
inverse_gudermannian(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.226191170883517

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Outside (-pi/2, pi/2) on the real line the imaginary part is pi:

#% cell
inverse_gudermannian(3.0)
#% output
#= 0.1420681583893965 + 3.141592653589793im

#% cell
inverse_gudermannian(-3.0)
#% output
#= -0.1420681583893965 + 3.141592653589793im

#% cell
n(inverse_gudermannian(1 + 2 im), 20)
#% output
#= 0.2275106584319431969469902 + 1.422911462459226796997833im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% cell
gudermannian(inverse_gudermannian(0.7))
#% output
#= 0.7

#% cell
gudermannian(inverse_gudermannian(3.0))
#% output
#= 3.0 - 0.0im

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gudermannian`, `atanh`, `tan`
